## 1. Inaccessible form: automated scan with axe-core (WCAG 2.0/2.1/2.2 A and AA rules)
   concept: axe runs in the real page in Chromium and reports each failed rule with the WCAG success criteria it maps to; the form is scanned empty and again after a failed submit, because errors are a different page state
   axe /bad -> 3 violations, 7 rules passed, 0 need review (out/axe-bad-empty.json)
     color-contrast   serious   WCAG 1.4.3        RGAA 3.2    3 nodes  Elements must meet minimum color contrast ratio thresholds
     html-has-lang    serious   WCAG 3.1.1        RGAA 8.3    1 nodes  <html> element must have a lang attribute
     label            critical  WCAG 4.1.2        RGAA 11.1   2 nodes  Form elements must have labels
   axe /bad -> 3 violations, 7 rules passed, 0 need review (out/axe-bad-errors.json)
     color-contrast   serious   WCAG 1.4.3        RGAA 3.2    3 nodes  Elements must meet minimum color contrast ratio thresholds
     html-has-lang    serious   WCAG 3.1.1        RGAA 8.3    1 nodes  <html> element must have a lang attribute
     label            critical  WCAG 4.1.2        RGAA 11.1   2 nodes  Form elements must have labels

## 2. Inaccessible form: what a screen reader gets after the failed submit
   concept: the fields have red borders and nothing else: no error text, no aria-invalid, no description. axe's label rule accepts a non-empty placeholder as a label (and Chromium uses it as the name), so these inputs pass, yet the error is colour only (WCAG 1.4.1) and never identified in text (3.3.1). Automated tools catch a minority of failures
   input[name=line1]    role=textbox    name="Address line 1"           description=""                                                         invalid=false
   input[name=city]     role=textbox    name="Town or city"             description=""                                                         invalid=false
   input[name=postcode] role=textbox    name="Postcode"                 description=""                                                         invalid=false
   input[name=country]  role=textbox    name="Country"                  description=""                                                         invalid=false
   4 inputs have the red .err border; focus after the reload is on: body
   markup: lang=(none) title="Form" <label>=0 fieldset+legend=0 autocomplete=0 buttons=0

## 3. Inaccessible form: keyboard only
   concept: a div with a click handler is not focusable and has no role, so Tab never reaches Save (WCAG 2.1.1, 4.1.2); with several text inputs and no submit button, Enter does not submit either (HTML implicit submission)
   Tab  1 -> textbox "Address line 1"
   Tab  2 -> textbox "Town or city"
   Tab  3 -> textbox "Postcode"
   Tab  4 -> textbox "Country"
   Tab  5 -> radio
   Enter in the postcode field: 0 POST requests, still on /bad

## 4. Accessible form: the same scan, empty and after a failed submit
   concept: lang on <html>, a <label> for every input, fieldset and legend around the address and the radios, autocomplete tokens, contrast at least 4.5:1, errors in text tied to their field with aria-describedby and aria-invalid, an error summary, and a page title that starts with Error:
   axe /good -> 0 violations, 17 rules passed, 0 need review (out/axe-good-empty.json)
   axe /good -> 0 violations, 24 rules passed, 0 need review (out/axe-good-errors.json)
   POST /good with nothing filled -> 422
   markup: lang=en title="Error: Change your address - Member portal" <label>=6 fieldset+legend=2 autocomplete=4 buttons=1

## 5. Accessible form: keyboard-only journey
   concept: Tab visits every control in reading order and the radio group is one tab stop; Enter on the button submits; after a failed submit focus lands on the error summary (role=alert, so it is also announced), each summary link moves focus to its field, and the corrected form submits with the keyboard alone
   Tab  1 -> textbox "Address line 1"
   Tab  2 -> textbox "Town or city"
   Tab  3 -> textbox "Postcode"
   Tab  4 -> textbox "Country"
   Tab  5 -> radio "From today"
   Tab  6 -> button "Save new address"
   focus on button "Save new address", press Enter
   -> 422, focus is now on #error-summary: alert "There is a problem"
   Tab -> link "Enter the first line of your address"
   Enter -> focus on #line1: textbox "Address line 1"

## 6. Accessible form: what a screen reader gets
   concept: the name comes from the <label>, the description joins the hint and the error message (aria-describedby), invalid comes from aria-invalid; the summary is an alert named by its heading
   #error-summary       role=alert      name="There is a problem"       description=""                                                         invalid=false
   #line1               role=textbox    name="Address line 1"           description="Error: Enter the first line of your address"              invalid=true
   #postcode            role=textbox    name="Postcode"                 description="For example, AB1 2CD Error: Enter a real postcode, like AB1 2CD" invalid=true
   #when-today          role=radio      name="From today"               description=""                                                         invalid=false
   typed the address, Space on "From today", Tab -> button "Save new address", Enter
   -> /good/done: "Address saved"

## 7. Findings by WCAG 2.2 success criterion, with the closest RGAA 4.1 criterion
   concept: automated rules found some failures; the rest needed a scripted keyboard journey and a look at the accessibility tree. An audit (RGAA or WCAG-EM) still needs a person with a screen reader
   html-has-lang              WCAG 3.1.1         RGAA 8.3        found by axe
   color-contrast             WCAG 1.4.3         RGAA 3.2        found by axe
   label                      WCAG 4.1.2         RGAA 11.1       found by axe (radios only; placeholders pass)
   colour-only error          WCAG 1.4.1         RGAA 3.1        found by accessibility tree
   error not announced        WCAG 3.3.1, 4.1.2  RGAA 11.10      found by accessibility tree
   no error summary or focus  WCAG 3.3.1, 2.4.3  RGAA 11.10      found by keyboard journey
   div as button              WCAG 2.1.1, 4.1.2  RGAA 7.3        found by keyboard journey
   radios not grouped         WCAG 1.3.1         RGAA 11.5, 11.6 found by markup query
   no autocomplete tokens     WCAG 1.3.5         RGAA 11.13      found by markup query

